        "event": "Frost Advisory",
        "headline": "Frost Advisory issued September 1 at 5:39AM AKDT until September 2 at 9:00AM AKDT by NWS Anchorage AK",
        "description": "* WHAT...Temperatures as low as 28 degrees will result in frost\nformation.\n\n* WHERE...Colder locations throughout the Anchorage Bowl and Lower\nMatanuska Valley.\n\n* WHEN...Through 9 AM AKDT Wednesday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Frost could harm sensitive outdoor vegetation. Sensitive\noutdoor plants may be killed if left uncovered.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...The cold airmass and clear skies observed this\nmorning are expected again tonight through early Wednesday morning.\nMany areas of Anchorage and the Matanuska V",
        "severity": "Minor",
        "certainty": "Likely",
        "urgency": "Expected",
        "sender": "NWS Anchorage AK",
        "effective": "2026-09-01T05:39:00-08:00",
        "expires": "2026-09-01T16:15:00-08:00",
        "areas": "Anchorage; Lower Matanuska Valley",
        "category": "Met",
        "response": "Prepare",
        "instruction": "Protect tender plants from the cold.",
        "lat": null,
        "lng": null
      },
      {
        "id": "urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.1b2bb1a13eb772f0915c82b1a1697d9a0e4bcc6b.001.1",
        "event": "Special Weather Statement",
        "headline": "Special Weather Statement issued September 1 at 2:30AM AKDT by NWS Fairbanks AK",
        "description": "* WHAT...Snow. Total snow accumulations of 3 to 6 inches above\n2000 feet and 1 to 3 inches below 2000 feet, with higher amounts\nabove pass level. Winds could gust as high as 30 mph over\nAtigun Pass Tuesday night.\n\n* WHERE...Central/Eastern Brooks Range and Eastern Arctic Coast.\n\n* WHEN...Tuesday September 1st through Wednesday September 2nd.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be difficult. Gusty winds could lead to\nareas of blowing snow which could significantly reduce\nvisibility.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS..",
        "severity": "Moderate",
        "certainty": "Observed",
        "urgency": "Expected",
        "sender": "NWS Fairbanks AK",
        "effective": "2026-09-01T02:30:00-08:00",
        "expires": "2026-09-01T16:00:00-08:00",
        "areas": "Eastern Beaufort Sea Coast; Central Brooks Range; Romanzof Mountains",
        "category": "Met",
        "response": "Execute",
        "instruction": "Slow down and use caution while traveling. Visit 511.alaska.gov or\ncall 5 1 1 for the latest road conditions.",
        "lat": null,
        "lng": null
      },
      {
        "id": "urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4e6b8a8d6773dab4b9a6831eaba864a5bc9f829f.001.1",
        "event": "Special Weather Statement",
        "headline": "Special Weather Statement issued August 31 at 5:10PM AKDT by NWS Anchorage AK",
        "description": "Strong northwesterly winds have developed across Shelikof Strait\nand the waters around southern Kodiak Island. These winds are\nresuspending ash from the 1912 Katmai-Novarupta eruption,\ncausing haze and potentially trace amounts of ash accumulation.\nThe villages of Karluk, Larsen Bay and Akhiok are most likely to\nbe impacted.\n\nThose in sensitive groups or with respiratory ailments are\nencouraged to avoid or reduce outdoor activity to avoid inhaling\nthe ash. The winds should weaken and shift brief",
        "severity": "Moderate",
        "certainty": "Observed",
        "urgency": "Expected",
